findings.summary:
Rule 5's new reason (IR3-5) is about linux-gnu: Rust std's statx and glibc's newfstatat. The EFFECT_GATE runtime base NATIVE_STATIC is 'Rust with musl, fully static, owner decision W01-AMEND-QW4' (allowlists.json runtimeBases.NATIVE_STATIC.description). On musl, read_to_end's metadata and position calls are fstat and lseek, both allowed. There, the hazard is an unbounded read, which rule 5's bound already forbids. The rule is right; only its reason should name the target. Separately, rule 5 now says the gate 'asks for no file metadata'. Two passages contradict it:
- the C1 row (line 229: 'The observation uses only these syscalls. openat, read, pread64, fstat, lseek and close');
- 'Still open' (lines 330-331: openat, pread64, fstat, lseek, 'which the observation also uses').
